  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An alkene has one double bond, so it carries two hydrogens fewer than the alkane with the same number of carbons: CₙH₂ₙ.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Unsaturated means that the molecule can take on more atoms — the double bond can open and add two new groups.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both carbons and the four atoms attached to them lie in one plane, with angles of about 120°.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Head-on overlap gives the strong σ bond; sideways overlap of the p orbitals gives the weaker π bond above and below the plane.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The loosely held π electrons are donated to the bromine, so the double bond opens and two new single bonds form.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bromine water loses its orange colour with an alkene and keeps it with an alkane.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The double bond is oxidised to a diol and the purple MnO₄⁻ is reduced to colourless Mn²⁺.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    But-1-ene, cis-but-2-ene, trans-but-2-ene and 2-methylpropene — four in all. The cis and trans forms are separate compounds.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    If either carbon carries two identical groups, the two arrangements are the same compound and there is no cis–trans pair.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Concentrated sulphuric acid at about 170 °C removes the elements of water — a dehydration — and gives ethene.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethene and steam over H₃PO₄ at about 300 °C and high pressure give ethanol.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen adds across the double bond, so C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    HCl adds across the double bond: C₂H₄ + HCl → C₂H₅Cl, chloroethane.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One chlorine atom goes to each carbon, so the product is ClCH₂CH₂Cl, 1,2-dichloroethane.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The double bonds open and the molecules join without losing any atom — addition polymerisation.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl, is vinyl chloride; its polymer is PVC.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Propene is CH₂=CHCH₃, so the repeat unit keeps the methyl group on every second carbon.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethyne, C₂H₂, is the first alkyne and has a carbon–carbon triple bond.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    CaC₂ + 2H₂O → C₂H₂ + Ca(OH)₂; the carbide is made by heating lime with coke in an electric furnace.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethyne is 92 % carbon, so its complete combustion releases a great deal of heat — about 3000 °C in oxygen.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each π bond can add bromine, so ethyne decolourises twice as much bromine as ethene per mole.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A triple bond removes four hydrogens relative to the alkane, so CₙH₂ₙ₋₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two carbons and the two hydrogens are collinear, so the molecule is linear.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen adds across the C=C bonds of the unsaturated chains, straightening them, so the product is solid.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Permanganate oxidises the double bond to two –OH groups, giving the diol, and is itself decolourised.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Breaking the chain leaves a carbon with a double bond in one of the fragments — the alkene.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One double bond adds one molecule of Br₂, so the ratio is one to one.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A triple bond is two π bonds, so each molecule of ethyne can add two molecules of bromine.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethene is the natural ripening hormone of plants, and it is used commercially to ripen bananas and tomatoes.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    PTFE, poly(tetrafluoroethene), is chemically inert, heat-resistant and has a very low friction surface.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The repeat unit is –CH₂–CH₂–, so the simplest ratio of atoms is CH₂, the same as the monomer's empirical formula.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The double bond opens and H and OH add across it, giving ethanol.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The double bond replaces two hydrogen atoms, so CₙH₂ₙ against CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two carbons and one double bond means ethene, C₂H₄.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethene is a feedstock, not a domestic fuel; LPG — propane and butane — is the cooking fuel.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One mole of ethene adds one mole of Br₂, so 0.20 mol reacts with 0.20 mol; M(Br₂) = 160 and m = 0.20 × 160 = 32 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The double bond is in a different position in the chain, so they are positional isomers.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both decolourise bromine water, but ethyne consumes twice as much per mole, so the volumes can distinguish them.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The C–C backbone is inert and no common organism has an enzyme to break it, so the bag remains in the soil or the sea.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The empirical unit has mass 14, and 42/14 = 3, so the molecular formula is (CH₂)₃ = C₃H₆ — propene.
  ]

]

